// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
; specs/analog/vacask_compare.t27 -- VACASK as a second reference simulator for the SAR blocks, next to ngspice: the comparator (M4b-psp) and the capacitive DAC (M4a)
; Analog, #10911 (epic #10486; the comparator is #10856, the DAC is #10540). An external EDA team suggested VACASK (an OSDI circuit simulator) as an independent
; validator. This file pins what both simulators measured on the SAME circuits with the SAME compiled PSP103 file, tabulates them against our models, and holds the
; tolerance at which they agree. A disagreement between our model and ngspice can now be attributed: to the simulator when VACASK sides with the model, to the model
; when VACASK sides with ngspice. It does not change the block specs; it `use`s them for the model column.
; -
; THE SECOND SIMULATOR. VACASK 0.3.4.dev3 (the PyPI wheel vacask-bin, built 2026-09-17, manylinux x86_64, pip-installed into a job directory inside a container on the
; remote host; the metadata says 0.3.4.dev3, the banner "This is vacask unknown."). The container image the external team's host carries has 0.3.2-103, a debug build that
; rejects the main-branch demo gilbert.sim at line 42 (a syntax error). 0.3.4.dev3 parses it and runs its op, dc-sweep, transient (0.43 s) and harmonic-balance (21.6 s)
; analyses, then stops in hbac1 with "Parameter 'truncharm' not found" and "Function 'sort' not found": the demo (fetched at run time, never stored here) is newer than the
; wheel (main was at f61a1637 on 2026-10-19). The 0.3.4 release tarball (the other candidate) does not start in that image: libopenblas.so.0 is missing and nothing may be
; installed there; the wheel carries its libraries. The comparator and the DAC need only transients, which this version runs. The wheel's copy of VACASK's IHP converter lacks
; one module (ng2vclib/m_inst_m.py), so the conversion of the PDK ran from the python package of a main-branch clone (python3 -m vacask.sg13g2tovc, the PDK COPIED first,
; because the converter writes into it, compiled with the wheel's own openvaf-r). The conversion took 12 s, no hand edit.
; -
; THE SAME MODEL FILE. The ngspice image's psp103.osdi (802248 bytes) is put first on VACASK's module path, so both simulators load that very file; the converter's own
; compile of the same Verilog-A source differs in bytes (826848) and was not used. PDK revision, corner (mos_tt), temperature (50 C) and the instance wrappers
; sg13_lv_nmos and sg13_lv_pmos are the PDK's own in both (the converter turns ngspice's .subckt into VACASK's subckt, same formulas, same parameters).
; -
; THE DECKS, data in specs/analog/decks/ (the golden SAR deck's subcircuits are GPL-2.0 and are translated at run time by an awk program described in the deck, never stored):
;   vacask_cmp_real.sim    the twin of sar_cmp_psp_real.cir: xb = the bare golden comparator with 4 fF on dp and dn, xtop = dynamic_comparator_top, an ideal 1.6 ps clock, 14 inputs
;                          (+-2 mV down to +-0.12 uV), the 6-point scan around xtop's flip point, and 8 finer points around VACASK's own
;   vacask_cdac_half.sim   the twin of sar_cdac_half.cir: one half of the split DAC, ideal capacitors, the 16 spot codes of sar_cdac.t27 (NG_SPOT)
; ngspice-47 ran the repository's own decks sar_cmp_psp_real.cir and sar_cdac_half.cir unchanged (the DAC deck with a 15-digit print) on the same host, the same day,
; 2026-10-20, in the image t27-ngspice:47-osdi (4 threads at most); its comparator numbers reproduce the committed pins of sar_comparator_psp.t27 digit for digit.
; -
; THE RESULT. Comparator, VDP - VDN = +2 mV (h = 16777), times after the clock's 50 % point (200.8 ps), 50 C, all PSP103 charge:
;   quantity                         ngspice-47     VACASK         our model         VACASK - ngspice          model against the simulators
;   decision (first output, 0.75 V)  523.032 ps     522.910 ps     5.350 ps          -0.122 ps (-0.023 %)      97.7 times too fast (no charge in the model)
;   resolution (|dp - dn|, 0.75 V)   1111.910 ps    1111.845 ps    7.186 ps          -0.065 ps (-0.006 %)      154.7 times too fast
;   Valid of xtop (0.75 V)           897.700 ps     897.567 ps     not modelled      -0.133 ps (-0.015 %)
; and over all 14 inputs (7 magnitudes, both signs): decision within 0.125 ps, resolution within 0.065 ps, Valid within 0.138 ps of ngspice; the winner at 3 ns the same in
; all 14 runs for xb and for xtop. The flip points (VDP - VDN at which dp and dn trade places):
;   xb, the bare latch    ngspice: inside +-2 units (+-0.12 uV)   VACASK: inside +-2 units   model: +0.12 uV (sp_flip, rounding)      all three at zero to 0.12 uV
;   xtop, with its loads  ngspice: -2570 .. -2540 units           VACASK: -2586 .. -2582    model: none (the loads are not modelled)  VACASK is 12 to 46 units
;                         (-0.15318 .. -0.15139 mV)                      (-0.15414 .. -0.15390 mV)                                       (0.7 to 2.7 uV, 0.5 to 1.8 %) lower
; The pre-clock waveform of xb's dp (a start that is not a solution, the same in both): at 0.05 ps 1.2197 V against 1.2509 V (31 mV apart: the first step), then within 0.34 mV
; from 1 ps and within 0.11 mV from 30 ps on (0.856297 V against 0.856334 V at 100 ps, 0.750878 V against 0.750903 V at 150 ps).
; Capacitive DAC, the top plate Vout, 16 codes, volts (ngspice's is the host run; ideal capacitors, V = 1.5 c / 1023):
;   code 1     ngspice 1.466275659813511e-3    VACASK 1.46627565982399e-3    closed form 1.466275659824047e-3   model within 1.74 units of 2^-24 V (1.04e-7 V) of it
;   code 341   ngspice 0.4999999999998767      VACASK 0.5                    closed form 0.5
;   code 1023  ngspice 1.499999999998824       VACASK 1.5                    closed form 1.5                    (code 0: ngspice -1.05e-14, VACASK 0, model -1 unit = -5.96e-8 V)
;   VACASK sits within 2e-15 V of the closed form at all 16 codes; ngspice's long run drifts up to 1.18e-12 V (at code 1023), the 1.29e-12 V drift sar_cdac.t27 saw. The VACASK
;   runs are 30 ns per code, ngspice's one 10 us run for all 1024 codes: the two are not the same experiment, so the drift is NOT attributed to ngspice, only not seen.
; -
; THE TOLERANCE, stated and kept apart from the difference it bounds. Decision and Valid time 0.15 ps, resolution time 0.08 ps (the observed maxima are 0.125, 0.138
; and 0.065, and each bound is checked from both sides: the data are not copies); the winners equal; the flip point of xtop within 46 units (2.7 uV, 1.8 % of the offset) and
; of the bare latch within 2 units; the pre-clock waveform within 0.4 mV from 1 ps; the DAC within 1.4e-12 V of ngspice, 2e-15 V of the closed form, and 1.2e-7 V of the model.
; THE DIFFERENCE EXPLAINED. The two flip points of xtop are 12 to 46 units apart. ngspice's own flip point moved by 10 units between reltol 1e-6 and 1e-8 (sar_comparator_psp.t27,
; "the tolerance is part of the number"); the two simulators control the local truncation error differently, and the gap is of that size, 1 % of the 0.153 mV offset. The decision
; and resolution times agree to 0.02 % and 0.006 %. The sign and the size of the offset, the thing the model cannot predict, are the circuit's: both simulators put the top's
; flip point 0.15 mV on dn's side.
; -
; WHAT THE SECOND SIMULATOR SETTLES. (1) The model's decision time is 97.7 times and its resolution time 154.7 times faster than the real-charge comparator, and VACASK says
; the same as ngspice to 0.03 %: the error is in the model (the devices' own charge is not there; the charge tables are #10777), not in ngspice. (2) The top's flip point
; of about -0.153 mV is the circuit's, not an ngspice artefact, and the model (an isolated latch) cannot have it. (3) The DAC's closed form is what an independent simulator
; gets too.
; -
; THE NEGATIVE CONTROLS, tests that pass by seeing the failure:
;   VACASK with tran_xmu = 0.25 (a more Euler-like integrator)   resolution time 0.65 to 1.87 ps early at all 7 inputs: at least 8 times the 0.08 ps bound
;   the plates left out of the ic list (uic starts an unlisted node at 0 V)   every DAC code 0.75 V high (the plate-to-top-plate capacitors start charged): 5e11 times the bound
;   a decision time moved by 1 ps; a flip point claimed equal to ngspice's bracket; the inputs exchanged (all 14 winners wrong)   each refused
; -
; WHAT A PASS SAYS. VACASK 0.3.4.dev3 and ngspice-47, on the same compiled PSP103 file, agree on the golden comparator's decision time to 0.125 ps (0.024 %), its resolution
; time to 0.065 ps (0.006 %), the Valid time of the top to 0.138 ps, every winner of 14 + 14 runs, the flip point of the bare latch to 0.12 uV and the top's to 2.7 uV, the pre-clock
; waveform to 0.34 mV, and the DAC to 1.2e-12 V at 16 codes; our models are the 98 and 155 times too fast and the 1.74-unit fixed-point DAC that sar_comparator_psp.t27 and
; sar_cdac.t27 say, against two simulators now.
; WHAT IT DOES NOT SAY. Anything about silicon: both are simulators of the same PSP103 file; a bug in that file or in the PDK parameters is in both. Another corner, temperature,
; W, L, supply or Monte Carlo mismatch. The DAC with real capacitors, the switches or the clock edge. The first 0.05 ps of the comparator start, where the two differ by 31 mV
; (a uic start from a state that is not a solution; the decision does not depend on it, the times agree). VACASK's own flip point of xtop was bracketed by 8 points, 4 units
; wide, not refined. A speed comparison: no wall time is claimed (VACASK took 72 s for its 28 runs, ngspice 311 s for its 20, the same host, 4 threads at most: different
; tolerance handling, step control and output, so not comparable). Harmonic balance, noise, or any analysis but the transient. The golden deck is read at run time and is not
; in this repository.
; ASCII only, English only.
; phi^2 + 1/phi^2 = 3 | TRINITY

module AnalogVacaskCompare;

